CAT = "nmlsEthics"; SEG = "ETH"
Q = [
("A lender sets higher minimum loan amounts in ZIP codes where most residents share one national origin, though incomes there match nearby areas. This is a form of:",
 "Redlining", ["Steering", "Blockbusting", "Loan flipping"],
 "Redlining is refusing or limiting credit in an area because of the race, national origin or other protected traits of its residents. It violates the Fair Housing Act and ECOA."),
("An originator steers a borrower who qualifies for a prime loan into a costlier subprime loan because it pays the originator more. This is:",
 "Steering, a predatory practice", ["Allowed if the borrower signs", "Allowed if the rate is disclosed", "Normal pricing for risk"],
 "Steering a consumer to a loan that is worse for them to earn more compensation is barred by Regulation Z's anti-steering rule and is a hallmark of predatory lending."),
("A lender applies the same minimum loan amount to every applicant, but the rule excludes far more applicants of one race without a business need. Under fair lending law this may be:",
 "Disparate impact", ["Disparate treatment", "Redlining", "Reverse redlining"],
 "Disparate impact is a neutral policy that falls more heavily on a protected group without a legitimate business justification. Disparate treatment is treating applicants differently because of a protected trait."),
("Which is a protected class under the Fair Housing Act but not under ECOA?",
 "Familial status", ["Race", "Religion", "National origin"],
 "The Fair Housing Act protects familial status and disability. ECOA covers marital status, age and public assistance income instead; race, religion and national origin are in both."),
("A borrower is repeatedly refinanced, paying new fees each time with little benefit. This predatory practice is:",
 "Loan flipping", ["Equity skimming", "Loan packing", "Redlining"],
 "Loan flipping is frequent refinancing that strips equity through repeated fees and points without a real benefit to the borrower."),
("An originator adds credit life insurance to a loan without the borrower's knowing consent. This practice is called:",
 "Loan packing", ["Loan flipping", "Rate locking", "Escrow padding"],
 "Packing adds unnecessary products such as single-premium credit insurance to the loan, raising the amount financed without real consent."),
("A buyer applies for an owner-occupied loan but plans to rent the home to tenants and never move in. This is:",
 "Occupancy fraud", ["A straw buyer scheme", "An air loan", "Identity theft"],
 "Misstating occupancy to get the lower rate and down payment of an owner-occupied loan is occupancy fraud."),
("A person with good credit buys a home in his name for a friend who cannot qualify, and the friend will live there and pay. The person is acting as a:",
 "Straw buyer", ["Non-occupant co-borrower", "Gift donor", "Power of attorney"],
 "A straw buyer lends their name and credit to hide the real borrower. A disclosed non-occupant co-borrower is legitimate; a hidden arrangement is fraud."),
("A loan is made on a property that does not exist, with a fake borrower and invented documents. This is called:",
 "An air loan", ["A silent second", "A bridge loan", "A flip"],
 "An air loan is fraud in which the borrower, property or both are fabricated, often with collusion from insiders."),
("A buyer gets an undisclosed second loan from the seller to cover the down payment the lender thinks the buyer paid. This is a:",
 "Silent second", ["Seller concession", "Gift of equity", "Buydown"],
 "A silent second is an undisclosed subordinate loan that hides the buyer's true leverage. Disclosed seller credits and gifts of equity are lawful."),
("Which is a red flag for employment fraud on a loan file?",
 "Employer phone is the borrower's", ["Two years at the same employer", "W-2s that match the pay stubs", "Pay that matches the occupation"],
 "Contact details for the employer that lead back to the borrower, or a verification returned by the borrower, suggest a fake employer. Consistent documents are normal."),
("A property sold for $180,000 two months ago and is now under contract for $315,000 with no major work done. This most suggests:",
 "Illegal flipping", ["A normal market increase", "A reverse mortgage", "A short sale"],
 "A rapid resale at a large markup with no documented improvements, often with an inflated appraisal, is a classic flip fraud red flag."),
("A borrower tells the originator she wants to leave a recent second job off the application because the income looks unstable. The originator should:",
 "Disclose all employment", ["Leave it off as she asks", "Raise the income to look stable", "List it as self-employment"],
 "The application must be accurate and complete. The originator can explain how unstable income is treated, but omitting or altering facts is misrepresentation."),
("An originator discovers after closing that the borrower's pay stubs were altered. The originator should:",
 "Report it to compliance", ["Say nothing since the loan closed", "Ask the borrower to repay the fees", "Delete the stubs from the file"],
 "Discovered fraud must be reported to the employer's compliance staff, who may file a SAR. Destroying records or staying silent compounds the violation."),
("A real estate agent asks the loan officer to share the buyer's income and credit score to help negotiate. The loan officer should:",
 "Decline without consent", ["Share it since the agent is involved", "Share only the credit score", "Share it by phone, not email"],
 "Nonpublic personal information is protected under GLBA and company policy. It may be shared only as the law allows or with the borrower's permission."),
("An originator emails a borrower's tax returns to her own personal email account to work from home. This is:",
 "A breach of data security duties", ["Fine if the account has a password", "Fine if deleted within a week", "Fine since she is the originator"],
 "The GLBA Safeguards Rule requires companies to protect customer information. Sending files to personal, unsecured accounts violates security policies and puts borrowers at risk."),
("A borrower files a complaint that fees were higher than quoted. The MLO's best first step is to:",
 "Follow the complaint process", ["Ignore it if the loan closed", "Offer a cash payment to drop it", "Ask the borrower to post a review"],
 "Complaints should be logged, investigated and answered under the company's procedures. Regulators expect documented complaint handling and may review it."),
("A title agent offers an originator a vacation trip for sending clients. Under RESPA, accepting it is:",
 "A prohibited kickback", ["Allowed if under a set value", "Allowed if disclosed to buyers", "Allowed for top producers"],
 "Any thing of value given for referrals of settlement business violates RESPA Section 8, whatever form it takes."),
("A broker charges the borrower a processing fee but does no processing work and passes part of it to another firm. Under RESPA this is:",
 "An unearned fee split", ["A normal processing charge", "An affiliated business fee", "A permitted marketing fee"],
 "RESPA bars splitting or accepting any portion of a settlement charge other than for services actually performed."),
("An originator advertises a low rate the company does not really offer, then moves callers into a costlier loan. This is:",
 "Bait-and-switch", ["Prequalification", "A rate lock", "A float-down option"],
 "Advertising or quoting terms that are not really available to lure a borrower in, then switching to worse terms, is deceptive and a prohibited act."),
("Under the Dodd-Frank Act, an act that takes unreasonable advantage of a consumer's lack of understanding of a product is:",
 "Abusive", ["Unfair", "Deceptive", "Lawful if disclosed"],
 "UDAAP defines abusive acts as those that materially interfere with understanding or take unreasonable advantage of a lack of understanding, inability to protect interests, or reasonable reliance."),
("A mortgage ad says \"No closing costs\" but the costs are added to the loan amount at closing. This ad is most likely:",
 "Deceptive", ["Abusive", "Accurate", "Exempt from rules"],
 "A representation that is likely to mislead a reasonable consumer about a material fact is deceptive under UDAAP and Regulation N."),
("A builder tells buyers they must use the builder's lender to get the advertised price, though a cheaper lender is available. The concern is:",
 "Coercion", ["Disparate impact", "Redlining", "Loan packing"],
 "Requiring buyers to use a particular settlement provider, or penalizing them for not doing so, raises coercion and RESPA concerns."),
("A borrower's bank statement shows a $14,000 deposit two weeks before application, and the borrower says a friend lent it. The originator should:",
 "Treat it as a debt and document it", ["Count it as the borrower's savings", "Call it a gift without a letter", "Leave the statement out of the file"],
 "A borrowed deposit is a liability that must be disclosed and counted. Calling it savings or a gift misrepresents the borrower's finances."),
("An originator is asked by a borrower whether to buy now or wait for rates to fall. The best ethical response is to:",
 "Explain options, no predictions", ["Promise rates will fall soon", "Tell the borrower to buy today", "Refuse to discuss rates at all"],
 "Originators may give general information and explain choices, but must not predict rates or pressure a decision, which could mislead the borrower."),
("A borrower signs a sales contract showing a price higher than the actual agreed price, with cash back to the buyer at closing. This is:",
 "Mortgage fraud", ["A seller concession", "A gift of equity", "A rate buydown"],
 "Inflating the price to fund undisclosed cash back to the buyer misrepresents the collateral and loan-to-value to the lender."),
("A borrower has the same address listed on several recent applications at different lenders, each claiming owner occupancy. This suggests:",
 "Shotgunning fraud", ["Normal rate shopping", "A refinance in progress", "Identity verification"],
 "Applying for several owner-occupied loans at once, each claiming to occupy a different home or hiding the other loans, is a fraud pattern known as shotgunning."),
("Which is an example of reverse redlining?",
 "Targeting minority areas for costly loans", ["Refusing to lend in a minority area", "Offering lower rates in every area of the city", "Pricing loans by credit score"],
 "Reverse redlining targets protected groups or areas for unfair, high-cost credit. Redlining refuses or limits credit there."),
("An originator is asked by the borrower's employer to share the loan status and approved amount. The originator should:",
 "Not share it without consent", ["Share it to verify employment", "Share only the loan amount", "Share it in writing only"],
 "Verifying employment does not authorize disclosing the borrower's loan details. Privacy law and company policy require consent."),
("An originator is paid more on loans closed with a title company owned by her spouse and does not tell the borrower. The ethical issue is:",
 "A conflict of interest", ["Normal referral practice", "A required affiliated fee", "A rate lock violation"],
 "Originators must disclose conflicts of interest, and financial ties to settlement providers may also trigger RESPA affiliated business and kickback rules."),
("A borrower's down payment source changes from savings to a gift a week before closing. The originator should:",
 "Update the file and source the gift", ["Keep the original asset list", "Tell underwriting after closing", "Have the donor take the gift back"],
 "Material changes must be reported to the lender and documented so the underwriter decides on accurate information."),
("An originator suggests to an appraiser that future orders depend on meeting contract prices. This violates:",
 "Appraisal independence rules", ["The Homeowners Protection Act", "The E-Sign Act", "The Red Flags Rule"],
 "Regulation Z's valuation independence rules prohibit coercing, extorting or rewarding an appraiser to hit a value, including tying future work to results."),
("A borrower's income documents show an employer whose business started six months ago but claims four years of employment there. This is a red flag for:",
 "Fake employment", ["Seasonal income", "Rental income", "Gift funds"],
 "Claimed tenure longer than the employer has existed signals fabricated employment or a fake employer and should be verified independently."),
("An originator tells a borrower the APR is the same as the note rate and hides the points to win the loan. This violates the duty to:",
 "Disclose costs truthfully", ["Lock the rate on request", "Order an appraisal promptly", "Explain escrow accounts"],
 "Ethical practice and TILA require accurate disclosure of loan costs. Hiding points and misstating the APR is deceptive."),
("A lender charges higher fees to older applicants because of their age, while their credit and income are the same as younger ones. Under ECOA this is:",
 "Prohibited age discrimination", ["Allowed for borrowers over 62", "Allowed as a risk adjustment", "Allowed if all fees are disclosed"],
 "ECOA bars discrimination based on age, provided the applicant can contract. A creditor may use age only in narrow ways, such as an empirically sound scoring system, never to charge more because of it."),
("A loan officer types a higher income into the application than the borrower stated. Who is responsible for the misrepresentation?",
 "The loan officer", ["Only the borrower who signed", "Only the underwriter", "No one, if the loan performs"],
 "Knowingly entering false information is fraud by the originator, even if the borrower signs and the loan performs."),
]
